WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.200
Few experiences in childhood have a more&nbsp;
enduring impact than the relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.400
we have with our siblings. Research&nbsp;
shows that having a good relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.200
with our brothers or sisters can&nbsp;
reduce feelings of loneliness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.360
depression, and anxiety. But a new&nbsp;
survey finds parental favoritism—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.480
perception that there was a favorite child&nbsp;
in the family—may negatively influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.440
sibling relationships. Think sibling rivalry.
Forty percent of Americans with siblings say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.800
their parents had a favorite. Why does this&nbsp;
matter? Lots of reasons, but here’s three:

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.480
First, it increases feelings of social&nbsp;
Isolation. Americans who report that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.840
parents had a favorite child report&nbsp;
feeling lonely more often growing up&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.840
Second, it damages sibling relationships. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.720
group also reports that they were not&nbsp;
as close to their siblings growing up.&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.720
Third, it undercuts trust in parents.&nbsp;
They are less likely to turn to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.920
parents when they had a problem growing up.
Finally, research has also shown that parental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.080
favoritism is universally problematic, and&nbsp;
not just for those who aren’t the favorite.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.960
Growing up, do you think your parents had&nbsp;
a favorite child? Let us know in our poll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.440
Also, let us know what other topics you’d&nbsp;
like our scholars to talk about in 60 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.560
and be sure to like and subscribe for&nbsp;
more research and videos from AEI.

